li='Decision panel | Cutoff: November 26, 2026, 6:36 a.m. CST (12:36 UTC)\n\n1. NVIDIA tutorial separates obstacle avoidance from a frozen walking policy\nPublished November 24, 2026. FACT: NVIDIA’s Microduck tutorial trains a separate obstacle-avoidance controller while keeping a walking policy fixed. Isaac Lab and Newton support simulation, sensor modeling, evaluation and deployment preparation; training includes noise, delay and missing measurements.\nIMPLICATION: For MATLAB trading research, separate a signal policy from execution and independent risk controls, and stress imperfect observations before deployment.\nUNCERTAINTY: A robotics tutorial, not evidence of trading profitability. Simulation and hardware behavior can differ; the architectural analogy requires financial validation.\nhttps://huggingface.co/blog/nvidia/microduck-obstacle-avoidance\n\n2. Graph-informed factor research highlights the cost hurdle in statistical arbitrage\nSubmitted November 25; listed November 26, 2026. FACT: The preprint builds a signed stock network after removing dominant correlation modes, then uses graph attention in a deep factor model. Its historical U.S. equity tests report improved residual-arbitrage performance, but net Sharpe becomes negative at five basis points per unit traded.\nIMPLICATION: Require an identity-graph ablation, equally tuned PCA benchmark and net-cost acceptance gate before adopting graph complexity.\nUNCERTAINTY: Unreplicated backtests covering 2015–2024. The headline results are gross of costs; borrow, impact, capacity and realistic fills remain implementation questions.\nhttps://arxiv.org/html/2611.34209v1\n\n3. Wall Street pares Wednesday’s losses before the Thanksgiving closure\nNovember 25, 2026 cash close; AP report 3:17 p.m. CST. FACT: AP reported Wednesday closes of about +0.3% for the Dow, −0.3% for the S&P 500 and −0.6% for the Nasdaq Composite after substantial intraday losses were pared.\nIMPLICATION: Evaluate Friday’s gap and liquidity afresh rather than treating Wednesday’s late recovery as an execution signal.\nUNCERTAINTY: Rounded reported index returns, not SPY/QQQ returns or live holiday-session prices. The intraday recovery does not establish a durable reversal.\nhttps://apnews.com/article/94fdb0ecb060ec65c0b02879ce0d2d09\n\n4. October PCE inflation reaches 3.8% year over year\nBEA released November 25, 2026, 7:30 a.m. CST. FACT: BEA reported October PCE inflation of 3.8% year over year and core PCE inflation of 3.3%. Both price indexes increased 0.3% from September.\nIMPLICATION: Stress rate-sensitive signals against persistent inflation and preserve release vintages; this is the result that was still unknown at yesterday’s cutoff.\nUNCERTAINTY: Economic estimates can be revised. The release does not by itself determine the next FOMC decision or the future inflation path.\nhttps://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-october-2026\n\n5. HP reports revenue growth while quarterly adjusted EPS declines\nReleased November 24, 2026; fiscal 2026 fourth-quarter results. FACT: HP reported fourth-quarter revenue growth of 9.3% year over year while non-GAAP EPS declined 10.8%. Its fiscal-year EPS measures included favorable tariff refunds.\nIMPLICATION: Test earnings quality and margins separately from top-line hardware demand; isolate nonrecurring items before extrapolating profitability.\nUNCERTAINTY: Company-reported results using both GAAP and non-GAAP measures. Revenue growth alone does not establish sustainable earnings growth or a stock-price response.\nhttps://www.hp.com/us-en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/hp-inc-reports-fiscal-2026-full-year-and-fourth-quarter-results.html\n\nVOLATILITY / EXECUTION: High, medium confidence: Nov. 25 prior-close VIX 28.29 (Yahoo Finance). Thanksgiving cash market closed. Friday cash opens 8:30 a.m., closes noon CST; eligible options close 12:15 p.m. Confirm contract and venue. Holiday futures hours, spreads, depth, volume, funding and gamma unverified. Six factor returns unavailable: publication-cleared comparable closes and rights unresolved; intended Nov. 24–25 and Oct. 28–Nov. 25 windows; no rankings.\n\nMATLAB / DRL: Independent capital-preservation gates; net-cost rewards; purged walk-forward regime tests. Align timetables, profile and preserve seeds. Check leakage, survivorship, look-ahead, corporate actions, stale options quotes, multiple testing, calibration and drift.\n\nRESEARCH QUESTION: Does a factor-stripped graph improve net mean-reversion over an identity graph and equally tuned PCA after spread, impact, borrow and turnover costs? Higher modeled costs eliminate the reported gross edge.\n\nFull dashboard and five graphics: https://github.com/rsh63/market-state-lab/blob/main/2026-11-26.md\n\nResearch and education, not individualized financial advice.\n\nWhich should a graph model beat first in your tests: identity-graph ablation or equally tuned PCA?\nEmail subscription: https://market-state-lab.ghost.io/?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=qmb_2026-11-26#/portal/signup'
